  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will result in tranquil conditions\nthrough Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure over the region today will shift offshore tonight\nand remain offshore through Wednesday. Overall, very pleasant\nand tranquil conditions are expected to continue through\nWednesday.\n\nClear skies and light winds will prevail for the rest of today \nwith highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Temps tonight will be \nwarmer than the last couple due to a combination of light \nsoutherly flow and increasing high clouds keeping temps steady in\nthe upper 50s to upper 60s. Wednesday's highs will be a few \ndegrees warmer than those of today as southwesterly flow ramps \nup in the afternoon. Breezy conditions are expected with winds \ngusting up to 20-25 mph for inland locations and up to 25-30 mph\nnear the coast.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross through the area on Thursday \nbringing the potential for a round of showers and storms.\n\nMid-level ridging will begin to break down on Wednesday across \nthe region as an upper-low traverses portions of the Hudson Bay\nand into Quebec. On Thursday, a more pronounced shortwave will \npass through the region, resulting in a cold front tracking\nthrough the area by Thursday night.\n\nSome shower activity is expected late Wednesday night into early\nThursday morning for mainly the Lehigh Valley and Pocono Region as\na decaying line of showers originating from the eastern Great \nLakes will be tracking toward the area. PoPs are around 20-40%\nduring this time period. Elsewhere, dry conditions are expected\nto continue under partly to mostly cloudy skies.\n\nOn Thursday, the cold front will sink south and east through the\narea throughout the course of the day. Not much in the way of \nsignificant impacts are expected to occur aside for a round of \nshowers and possibly some isolated thunderstorms in the \nafternoon and early evening from north to south. The severe \nweather threat is quite low at this time mainly due to limited\ninstability with MLCAPEs around 500 J/kg, poor lapse rates and\nbulk shear less than 30 kt. This is supported by the Storm\nPrediction Center which only has the southern half of our area\nin General Thunder. There does appear however, to be some sort\nof a hydro threat mainly further south where the Weather\nPrediction Center has a Marginal Excessive Rainfall Outlook \nover portions of the area. Any precipitation should diminish\nafter midnight as the cold front tracks through the area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Another disturbance will approach the area on\nSaturday night into Sunday causing another round for showers \nand storms.\n\nBehind the front, a drier and more comfortable airmass looks to\nsettle into the region for Friday and into at least the start \nof Saturday as high pressure temporary settles in. \n\nBeyond the first half of Saturday though, the forecast becomes \na bit more uncertain due to a stalled frontal boundary off the \nsouthern Mid-Atlantic coast with waves of low pressure riding \nalong it. If this is to come to fruition, there may be several \nperiods of unsettled weather for Saturday night into Sunday. At \nthis point, kept close to the NBM which carries 20-40% PoPs \nSaturday night through Sunday night. This will continue to be \nmonitored over the next several days, but it does appear it will\nbecome more humid over the weekend.",
